[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9de8e87-355b-535c-8bee-af438d935463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 180 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4})^{t/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216508} {:answer/id #uuid "1771708f-0db0-5a1b-88ed-52bd8e3a8c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 120 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4})^{t/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216509} {:answer/id #uuid "17ae1a46-8ac8-585a-9813-5ecc5267b67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 180 \\cdot (\\frac{4}{5})^{t/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216510} {:answer/id #uuid "260fcae8-73b1-5741-b3e7-cb624ddbe825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 120 \\cdot (\\frac{4}{5})^{t/5} + 60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216511} {:answer/id #uuid "56894a87-caf1-5368-b7da-ab7cdc64572e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 120 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4})^{t/5} + 60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216512}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a729450a-3613-587e-b6d4-fa83b92a81fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216507}] :question/id #uuid "3a0ceb08-370a-56da-9aeb-5886527d5b49" :question/math-academy-id "q-327450" :question/problem "In a climate-controlled lab, a heated ceramic tile was placed on a counter in a room that maintained a constant temperature of $60^{∘}F$. At $t = 0$ minutes, the tile’s temperature was $180^{∘}F$. After $5$ minutes, its temperature had dropped to $150^{∘}F$, and after $10$ minutes, it was $127.5^{∘}F$. The tile continued to cool over time.\n\nWhich of the following functions best models the temperature $T(t)$ in degrees Fahrenheit of the tile $t$ minutes after it was placed on the counter?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99e05855-8ea8-5f99-9fb4-fe5bfb1a182e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 128 \\cdot (\\frac{7}{8})^{t/5} + 66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216514} {:answer/id #uuid "a95ab12b-e16b-546e-b99b-e9e77c1f8614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 128 \\cdot (\\frac{5}{6})^{t/5} + 66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216515} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5c5c28-0c42-5164-950c-f0a8d4dff033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 128 \\cdot (\\frac{7}{8})^{t/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216516} {:answer/id #uuid "0eb7cf2e-4063-5338-be97-2972ea767410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 194 \\cdot (\\frac{7}{8})^{t/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216517} {:answer/id #uuid "23cc23ac-9948-5249-bc71-0ed899803769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(t) = 194 \\cdot (\\frac{5}{6})^{t/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216518}] :answer-field/id #uuid "daec1266-a9dd-5bb0-8df2-1707a183033f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216513}] :question/id #uuid "f7bc92b8-fc52-59ba-a433-20edfd6be299" :question/math-academy-id "q-327458" :question/problem "In a data center, a warm heat sink was placed on a tray in a room that maintained a constant temperature of $66^{∘}F$. At $t = 0$ minutes, the heat sink’s temperature was $194^{∘}F$. After $5$ minutes, its temperature had dropped to $178^{∘}F$, and after $10$ minutes, it was $164^{∘}F$. The heat sink continued to cool over time.\n\nWhich of the following functions best models the temperature $T(t)$ in degrees Fahrenheit of the heat sink $t$ minutes after it was placed on the tray?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e4329c-f59e-50af-82f6-bed8f41a1de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305499} {:answer/id #uuid "84b0163c-45e3-514d-a83c-ab39e8e00fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306415} {:answer/id #uuid "a11ad399-54da-5b14-9b47-556b3e072d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306663} {:answer/id #uuid "bc87a16a-0e2e-5b54-9e49-126ddd8e00ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306908} {:answer/id #uuid "d64d68b5-2e8c-527d-ae3e-aa8790d57073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a11ad399-54da-5b14-9b47-556b3e072d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306663} :answer-field/id #uuid "79eaab3d-a2a0-5b82-bfca-f63fc030ff15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a266b256-994a-5563-82c8-322702645919" :question/math-academy-id "q-334398" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{p}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+2p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-2p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+{p}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2p)p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2p \\\\ y &=-{p}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80000d4c-7739-5f64-a737-2ea473eeaf24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309751} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ef16fa-44ab-5bb3-afcb-42dbcf98c3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7ef16fa-44ab-5bb3-afcb-42dbcf98c3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310721} :answer-field/id #uuid "03fdb7f2-eca0-5406-840d-1205920d819e" :answer-field/key "fie