d #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315481} {:answer/id #uuid "92e83496-3189-5e03-8cde-441b6833ff0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315939} {:answer/id #uuid "dc308651-5eb0-524d-b97d-db4942257375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316343} {:answer/id #uuid "e52ac4aa-e503-5d1f-b967-da2347d2b2d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f6dbc93-1b83-5d25-acd9-9c2c8535de5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315481} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f03cf21-0038-533a-aab9-c6147ed7f920" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "637c8c06-b50d-580c-8555-a7f2b7fa591e" :question/math-academy-id "q-342023" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=8x$ defined on $[-2,2).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $4$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=4{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n8x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-2,2).$ \nTherefore, the $4$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}8t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ [4{t}^{2}]_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "363a3784-86cd-5624-ae01-ecfc85592e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315435} {:answer/id #uuid "e786c74c-45b3-56a6-9fc8-0de482bd1c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316399} {:answer/id #uuid "ff24cf77-0eb8-54be-85c8-fada8a265238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "363a3784-86cd-5624-ae01-ecfc85592e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315435} :answer-field/id #uuid "562b01d0-1c00-5656-95e5-e94e170b26e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316746} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b1ab9d6-d88f-54db-8898-c8776929e416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315179} {:answer/id #uuid "623cedf2-3888-5ff9-ac59-414dab43a963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (n\\pi +5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315672} {:answer/id #uuid "7f98be46-8f13-5c49-8b77-7ff5e985b64e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25(-1)^{n}}{n{\\pi }^{2}(n\\pi +5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b1ab9d6-d88f-54db-8898-c8776929e416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315179} :answer-field/id #uuid "aad26612-844b-5860-be9b-50e6ab9948ab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7431cef2-4a56-5a41-81e1-4185e67efb64" :question/math-academy-id "q-342203" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))+{b}_{n}\\cdot n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({b}_{n}n\\pi +5{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-{a}_{n}n\\pi +5{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2} &=0 \\\\ {b}_{n}n\\pi +5{a}_{n} &=0 \\\\ -{a}_{n}n\\pi +5{b}_{n} &=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the second equation, \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the third equation gives\n\n$$\n-(-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5})n\\pi +5{b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\cdot \\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+25)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b9914c2-c267-5af4-b108-3360dccddb44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315985} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e67bb4-c548-5d4b-b417-1b027f6f9f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316076} {:answer/id #uuid "d7906531-8bc2-5207-b0f1-73878255829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b9914c2-c267-5af4-b108-3360dccddb44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315985} :answer-field/id #uuid "9835d1ba-3f1d-500f-a45d-e5d7f26272f5" :answer-