s $3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed2ecc16-47db-5546-94f2-1abddae9b698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186224639} {:answer/id #uuid "feeb59bc-d5e6-5a26-9450-62dc6628ea41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186224640} {:answer/id #uuid "58aa9a0e-f9de-5ce8-b8f7-d10fb5770530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186224641} {:answer/id #uuid "6136fa53-8a9a-52dd-9673-90d6b234beff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186224642} {:answer/id #uuid "7265e3a7-c17e-53d0-9060-e00b98d970e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186224643}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2e8a374a-c3ec-5542-8c16-b74cc0ed8b9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186224638}] :question/id #uuid "376764dd-430e-5250-bba1-8e1b4ed6d25c" :question/math-academy-id "q-323464" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = 3(\\frac{1}{4})^{x} + 1$ on the domain $x \\ge 0$. Which of the following statements is true?\n\n1. $f$ is increasing on its domain.\n2. $f$ does **NOT** have a minimum value.\n3. $f$ does **NOT** have a maximum value."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "c3892d3c-453b-55c9-ad9f-b84e5d46520c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186224653}] :question/id #uuid "173b0388-a13d-53a6-b690-53b99688a779" :question/math-academy-id "q-323533" :question/problem "What is the maximum value of $f(x) = 9(\\frac{1}{3})^{x} + 2$ for $x \\ge 0$?The maximum value of $f$ is\n{{field-1}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186225860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "fbd4aa27-ac8b-57e1-aadc-09eec25cbe54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186224655}] :question/id #uuid "6c8ee46b-656a-536d-925a-0766fbb4c60b" :question/math-academy-id "q-323549" :question/problem "What is the maximum value of $f(x) = 8(\\frac{1}{2})^{x} + 6$ for $x \\ge 3$?The maximum value of $f$ is\n{{field-1}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228784 :question/id #uuid "e80a46e5-d2ab-5fdc-be40-47a460179ecc" :question/math-academy-id "e-20602" :question/problem "A maglev train accelerates at a rate of $5.6 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$. What is this acceleration rate, in miles per minute squared, rounded to the nearest tenth?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{mi} = 1,609\\,\\textrm{m}$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nWe're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{s}^2$ into $\\textrm{min}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{s}^2}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The acceleration rate given in the problem statement is $5.6 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$.\n- We are given that there are $1,609$ meters per mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,609}$ miles per meter.\n- There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n$\\dfrac{60\\,\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}$\nTo determine the number of seconds squared in one minute squared, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$(\\frac{60s}{min})^{2}|= \\frac{60^{2}s^{2}}{min^{2}}; = \\frac{3, 600s^{2}}{min^{2}}$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 5.6 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = 5.6 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{m}}} \\cdot  3 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 5.6 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\cdot  3 , 600 ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{5.6 \\cdot  3 , 600}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{20 , 160}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  12.5 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nTherefore, the maglev train is accelerating at a speed of approximately\n\n$$\n12.5\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228785 :question/id #uuid "872d0931-7cb1-553c-b669-a879bd1e9782" :question/math-academy-id "e-20603" :question/problem "A professional sports complex covers $3,872,000 \\, \\textrm{yd}^2$. What is the area of the complex in square miles? Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{mi} = 1,760\\,\\textrm{yd}$" :question/worked-solution "We want to convert the area from square yards to square miles.\n\nWe are given that there are $1,760$ yards per mile, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,760 \\, \\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{mi}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1, 760yd}{mi})^{2} &= \\frac{1, 760^{2}yd^{2}}{mi^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3, 097, 600yd^{2}}{mi^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of area $(\\textrm{yd})^2$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{mi})^2$, we need to multiply $3,872,000 \\, \\textrm{yd}^2$ by the reciprocal of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}^2}{3,097,600 \\, \\textrm{yd}^2}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $3,872,000 \\, \\textrm{yd}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 3 , 872 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{yd}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 3 , 872 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{yd}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{mi}}^{2}}{3 , 097 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{yd}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 3 , 872 , 000 \\cancel{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{mi}}^{2}}{3 , 097 , 600 \\cancel{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 , 872 , 000}{3 , 097 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mi}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.25 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mi}}^{2} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the area is approximately $1.25\\,\\textrm{mi}^2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228786 :question/id #uuid "c971c3ec-1cdd-5de5-b271-1dd9c31f469f" :question/math-academy-id "e-20604" :question/problem "An archaeological site spans $1.1\\,\\textrm{mi}^2$. What is the total area of the site in square yards?\n\nHint: $1\\,\\textrm{mi} = 1,760\\,\\textrm{yd}$" :question/worked-solution "We want to convert the area of the site from square miles to square yards.\n\nWe are told that there are $1,760\\,\\textrm{yd}$ per $\\textrm{mi}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,760\\,\\textrm{yd}}{\\textrm{mi}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1, 760yd}{mi})^{2} &= \\frac{1, 760^{2}yd^{2}}{mi^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3, 097, 600yd^{2}}{mi^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $1.1\\,\\textrm{mi}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 1.1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mi}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 1.1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mi}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 , 097 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{yd}}^{2}}{ {\\text{mi}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.1 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{mi}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 , 097 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{yd}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{mi}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.1 \\cdot  3 , 097 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{yd}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 3 , 407 , 360 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{yd}}^{2} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the site is $3,407,360\\,\\textrm{yd}^2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228834 :question/id #uuid "99d2ed59-3062-55d3-b57a-b4d1e04fd4b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-21383" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "We need to write the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{8}\\left(2^{-s}\\right) - \\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the form\n\n$$\nk\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nFactoring out the common factor of\n\n$$\n\\left(2^{-s}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{8}(2^{-s}) - (2^{-s}) &= \\frac{7}{8} \\cdot (2^{-s}) - 1 \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - 1) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{7}{8} - \\frac{8}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{1}{8}) \\cdot (2^{-s}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(2^{-s})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228835 :question/id #uuid "2682674b-095f-52ee-819b-75bef64bd999" :question/math-academy-id "e-21384" :question/problem "Express $\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t$ as a power of $\\dfrac43$ only." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the rules of exponents:\n\n- Negative exponents: $x^{-n} = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n- The power rule: $\\left(a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nNow, our expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nhas an additional factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{64}{27}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that\n\n$$\n64=4^3 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 27=3^3\n$$\n\nare both perfect cubes. Therefore, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4^3}{3^3}\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nBy the power of the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^3\\left(\\dfrac43\\right)^t\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{4}{3})^{3}(\\frac{4}{3})^{t} &= (\\frac{4}{3})^{t + 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228860 :question/id #uuid "eb690bf5-082a-5ae1-90b2-c0214553331a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21419" :question/problem "![](images/11/11105ba18d0d1fec18f48309e2ec378d316e3357252a7a8e1328d2d6c8fa3302.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows an approximate trend line for the relationship between two variables $x$ and $y$ in a dataset $R$.\n\nA new dataset $S$ is formed by multiplying the $x$-coordinate of every data point of $R$ by $2$. Which of the following could be an equation of a trend line for dataset $S$?\n\n1. $y=1.5x + \\dfrac 2 3$\n2. $y=0.75x+2$\n3. $y=0.75x+\\dfrac 2 3$\n4. $y=0.5x+2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an approximate trend line for $R$ from the plot.\n\n![](images/9a/9a21a7f3d6768769f0884dd4a2c712f05cd6e62002569abe9ab593fa5f1b9c30.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through the points\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) \\approx (4,8)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2,y_2) \\approx (12,20)\n$$\n\nSo, an estimate of the slope $m$ of the line is\n\n$$\nm \\mid \\approx \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}}; = \\frac{20 - 8}{12 - 4}; = \\frac{12}{8}; = 1.5\n$$\n\nSo, an approximate trend line, in slope-intercept form, is\n\n$$\ny=1.5x+b\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is the $y$-intercept to be estimated. We do this by substituting in the coordinates of a point on the line, say $(4,8){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 1.5x + b \\\\\n8 &= 1.5(4) + b \\\\\n8 &= 6 + b \\\\\n2 &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the trend line for dataset $R$ is\n\n$$\ny=1.5x+2\n$$\n\nWe're told that dataset $S$ is formed by multiplying the $x$-coordinate of every data point of $R$ by $2$.\n\nLet $x_R$ be a $x$-value in $R$ and $x_S$ be the corresponding $x$-value in $S$. Then, we have\n\n$$\nx_S = 2 x_R\n$$\n\nSubsituting\n\n$$\nx_R = \\dfrac {x_S} 2\n$$\n\ninto the equation for $R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{S} &= 1.5(\\frac{x_{S}}{2}) + 2 \\\\\n&= 0.75x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, a suitable line of best fit for $S$ is\n\n$$\ny=0.75x+2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **only II.**"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228913 :question/id #uuid "81f19562-1aee-5d9c-955c-4de09467906f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21717" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-1\\right)^2 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nIf $y'(x) = t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\left( \\dfrac{2\\ln|t| - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2} \\right) f(t) + g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny = xf(y') + g(y'),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of $y'.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution $y'(x) = t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + \\dfrac{2x}{t-1} = \\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} + P(t)x = Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1+t}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x & =\\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{1-{t}^{2}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{2\\ln|t| - t^2+C}{2(t-1)^2}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny = x(t-1)^2 + 2t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-{t}^{2}+C}{2(t-1)^{2}})(t-1)^{2}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=(t-1)^2,\\ g(t)=2t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t) = t^2 +1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228925 :question/id #uuid "e71f19d1-391d-5c39-8fd6-c7adb8dd8a70" :question/math-academy-id "e-21749" :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(8\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g = 16\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$ Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(9\\times10^{4})\\,\\textrm{m/s},$ where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\nF_\\text{in} = G\\,\\dfrac{mM}{r^2},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-F_\\textrm{in}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, \n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac{k}{r^2},\n$$\n\n with constant of proportionality $k=GM > 0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n-g = -\\dfrac{k}{R^2} \n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk = gR^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}r} = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet $v_0$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R) = v_0,$ we can solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\nv^2 = \\dfrac{2gR^2}{r} + v_0^2 - 2gR.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n$R=(8\\times10^5),$\n$g=16,$ and\n$v_0=(9\\times10^{4}),$\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 16\\cdot (8\\times {10}^{5})^{2}}{r}+(9\\times {10}^{4})^{2}-2\\cdot 16\\cdot (8\\times {10}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2048\\times {10}^{10}}{r}+(81\\times {10}^{8})-(256\\times {10}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.048\\times {10}^{13}}{r}+(8.1\\times {10}^{9})-(0.0256\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.048\\times {10}^{13}}{r}+((8.1-0.0256)\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.048\\times {10}^{13}}{r}+(8.0744\\times {10}^{9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228926 :question/id #uuid "95939a44-8f36-59a8-80a8-1fd81d5f89a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21750" :question/problem "Consider a planet with radius $(1.2\\times10^{8})\\,\\textrm{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g = 18.9\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$\nWhat is the escape velocity of an object launched radially outward from the planet's surface? Assume only the force due to gravity acts on the object, \n\nExpress your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate." :question/worked-solution "Escape velocity is the minimum launch speed from a planet’s surface required to escape its gravity and reach infinity, neglecting all forces except gravity.\n\nLet $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\nF_\\text{in} = G\\,\\dfrac{mM}{r^2},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, $M$ is the planet's mass, and $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-F_\\textrm{in}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, \n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac{k}{r^2},\n$$\n\n with constant of proportionality $k=GM > 0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n-g = -\\dfrac{k}{R^2} \n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk = gR^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}r} = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet $v_0$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R) = v_0,$ we can solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\nv^2 = \\dfrac{2gR^2}{r} + v_0^2 - 2gR.\n$$\n\nFor the object to escape the planet's gravity, we require a positive initial velocity, and for the velocity to never equal zero.\n\nNotice in our velocity equation that as $r \\to \\infty,$ the expression $\\dfrac{2gR^2}{r}$ approaches zero. So, for the velocity to never vanish, we must have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR & \\ge 0 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & \\ge 2gR \\\\ {v}_{0} & \\ge \\sqrt{2gR}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum velocity of the object to escape the surface of the planet is\n\n$$\nv_e = \\sqrt{2gR}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values $R=(1.2\\times10^{8})$ and $g=18.9,$ we conclude that the escape velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{e} & =\\sqrt{2\\cdot (1.2\\times {10}^{8})\\cdot 18.9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2\\cdot 1.2\\cdot 18.9)\\times {10}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45.36}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{8}} \\\\  & ≈(6.73\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three significant figures."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228930 :question/id #uuid "2f5d8d59-2cc7-53bf-92ad-6062df331851" :question/math-academy-id "e-21765" :question/problem "A water-treatment holding tank initially holds $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid containing $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved coagulant.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of coagulant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which coagulant enters the tank minus the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of the coagulant enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *coagulant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which coagulant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which coagulant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $700\\,\\textrm{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}$ and leaves at $20\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n700 + 30t - 20t = (700+10t)\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of coagulant in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\textrm{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *coagulant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{700+10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q=1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(70+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|70+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(70+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (70+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(70+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q) & =(70+t)^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\ (70+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(70+t)Q & =1.5(70+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2}) & =1.5(70+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 1.5(70+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(70+t)^{2} & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)+\\frac{C}{(70+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $12\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of coagulant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 12,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =12 \\\\ 0.5(70+0)+\\frac{C}{(70+0)^{2}} & =12 \\\\ 35+\\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =-23 \\\\ C & =-23(70)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.5(70+t)-\\frac{23(70)^{2}}{(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.5(70+t)-23{(\\frac{70}{70+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of coagulant in the tank $8$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8) & =0.5(70+8)-23{(\\frac{70}{70+8})}^{2} \\\\  & =39-23{(\\frac{70}{78})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈20.48\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228931 :question/id #uuid "bbf602f0-1101-55d5-a6b3-9bfc5731e9ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-21766" :question/problem "A syrup mixing tank in a confectionery factory can hold up to $240\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of syrup solution with $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of dissolved sugar. A more concentrated syrup with concentration $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.5\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =36\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $96\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t) = 96 + 24t - 16t = 8(12+t)\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{8(12 + t)}\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{8(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =36-\\frac{2Q}{12+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{2}{12 + t}\\,Q = 36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(12+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|12+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t) = (12+t)^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{12+t}\\,Q) & =(12+t)^{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\ (12+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(12+t)Q & =36(12+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2}) & =36(12+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 36(12+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(12+t)^{2} & =36\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(12+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(12+t)+\\frac{C}{(12+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $32\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 32,$ and solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ 12(12+0)+\\frac{C}{(12+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 12\\cdot 12+\\frac{C}{(12)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{12}^{2}} & =-112 \\\\ C & =-112(12)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(12+t)-\\frac{112(12)^{2}}{(12+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =12(12+t)-112{(\\frac{12}{12+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $240\\,\\textrm{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V(t_o) = 240$ to solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =240 \\\\ 96+24{t}_{o}-16{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 96+8{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =144 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =18\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(18) & =12(12+18)-112{(\\frac{12}{12+18})}^{2} \\\\  & =360-112{(\\frac{12}{30})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈342.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228943 :question/id #uuid "38be687e-4fea-5e8a-a810-4c7f50667ea8" :question/math-academy-id "e-21842" :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n![](images/e7/e70e54b6a70cebd480b86baf9d0e1d90bde0b3d9277c982d5d5e77218d0d2f5d.png)" :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n![](images/48/48965cee8a09df4f637b4d997fdb9c90673e69a90ea1320434f9b471a2fed280.png)\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{2t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{(2-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s \\leq 0.$\n\nFor $s > 0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{(2-s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-s}{[{e}^{(2-s)t}]}_{0}^{3}+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{-{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3(2-s)}-1}{2-s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228955 :question/id #uuid "f8786fd7-c9d5-5ada-9aa0-faa2a45f4cd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6641" :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s) = \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ for $f(t) = u(t - 2) + u(t-6),$ where $u$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise functions $u(t-2)$ and $u(t-6),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2) & ={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 2\\end{aligned} \\\\ u(t-6) & ={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & t<6 \\\\ 1,\\, & t\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/6d/6ddb2983dec1f3221778a336bb6ef3cce3138476b67d64334ab6660184a79f95.png)\n\nSo, for our Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second integral diverges for $s \\leq 0.$\n\nFor $s > 0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{6}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{6}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{2}^{6}+2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{6}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-6s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}(0-{e}^{-6s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-6s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-6s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} =\\dfrac{e^{-2s}}{s} +\\dfrac{e^{-6s}}{s}, \n\\qquad \ns > 0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a734bcb5-0889-5e52-9c84-899998977142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{x + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226328} {:answer/id #uuid "cb688c3e-1b8b-5a79-a546-7c56d5e0566e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/((x + 2)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226329} {:answer/id #uuid "e673442f-cbc5-578a-9d8f-fe8e04e46941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3)/((x + 2)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226330} {:answer/id #uuid "2794f978-8a03-5a48-ae54-434b809ea095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/((x + 2)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226331} {:answer/id #uuid "6e503cd1-0fe3-57b2-8903-c70d055debb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2(x + 2)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226332}] :answer-field/id #uuid "60dc04fc-a16e-57c0-995a-3984cdd04362" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226327}] :question/id #uuid "670ff0d6-532d-5dd5-991c-e75d2fe91b3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-277403" :question/problem "Which expression is equivalent to $((\\frac{12}{x + 2}))/(6x + 12)$, where $x \\ne - 2$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92300746-c59f-5dfb-9c22-74a4630152f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(14)/((x - 5)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226334} {:answer/id #uuid "adba4dfe-8ef0-5391-8a1b-70bcd9653ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/((x - 5)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226335} {:answer/id #uuid "f3fd93e8-0130-5a6a-9185-627754dd61c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7)/((x - 5)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186226336} {:answer/id #uuid "f49b51ab-06a8-5b69-9049-234bcb3