answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3})^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227587} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca7ae6b-758f-5e45-ab70-32b01a2b22cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3})^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227588}] :answer-field/id #uuid "27831fc1-bde8-5190-8e47-c3f634d6b6a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227583}] :question/id #uuid "0290ad43-8cee-5a12-a565-921ef9a6160d" :question/math-academy-id "q-325227" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is equivalent to $3^{2k}4^{k}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229630 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b2271ff-21bf-51b4-8d45-2c4647b83718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b2271ff-21bf-51b4-8d45-2c4647b83718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302533} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fc5293e-1c4c-59c9-887f-e438c1d0b4b5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304194} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64825e76-f86a-56d6-92a0-e1d80a038471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64825e76-f86a-56d6-92a0-e1d80a038471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303068} :answer-field/id #uuid "a50aeb11-4c90-521c-83b1-4ae8839287ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "390843f1-e8b0-5115-a3d2-290299a8ce3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332446" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-6\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +6\\beta &=4\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3},\\beta =\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(-\\frac{1}{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(\\frac{1}{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e9a1c25-344d-5dbd-be20-eff2b5d788b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e9a1c25-344d-5dbd-be20-eff2b5d788b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303243} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2d0d86-dec6-5402-9728-c6d1665a8d04" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2293beab-4d00-5f11-b2f4-e3ae5bdd1a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2293beab-4d00-5f11-b2f4-e3ae5bdd1a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302674} :answer-field/id #uuid "af29e177-a4de-503a-a535-0de11fe62c50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7dd8dc08-2fc9-5908-9810-58211c6eb3df" :question/math-academy-id "q-332793" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-9y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{3x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta -9\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta -9\\alpha &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -9\\beta &=6\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{13},\\beta =-\\frac{6}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{6}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+(-\\frac{4}{13})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-\\frac{6}{13})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"}]
